  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1028 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nQuiet weather is in the forecast for Tuesday before showers return\nWednesday morning. Weak WAA ahead of an upper trough will provide\nthe moisture and lift to justify chance pops (30 to 50% chance)\nstarting in the morning hours, but initially concentrated to far\nnorthern Wisconsin, closer to the trough. The scattered shower\nactivity is then expected to quickly spread across the region as\nthe cold front sweeps across the area. Winds will shift northwards\nbehind the front, providing the setup for some lake effect showers\nin the typical lake effect areas of north-central Wisconsin and\npossibly portions of the Lake Michigan shoreline, especially \nnorthern Door County. These showers will likely (50-70% chance) \nstick around while the more northerly flow persists much of\nThursday before tapering to an end over the day Friday. Overnight\nlow temperatures may suffice for snow to mix in at times in the\nlake effect regions. Precipitation totals have come up slightly\nacross the region, but overall the on and off showers Wednesday\nand Thursday will still be fairly light. Probabilistic guidance\npaints out around 40-60% chance of exceeding a tenth of an inch\nacross the area, and around 20-30% of a quarter of an inch across\nthe northern portions of Wisconsin. The lake effect areas may also\nneed monitoring, as there is some potential to see locally higher\nrainfall totals if precipitation persists for the entire period,\nespecially in north-central Vilas County and in northern Door near\nWashington Island. At this time, probabilistic guidance highlights\nthese two areas around 30% chance of exceeding a half an inch of\nrainfall. Snowfall amounts have not changed much as the window for\nflurries remains small and accumulations will likely remain\nminimal on the surface.\n\nTemperatures will see a return to near normal with the influx of\ncooler air, with highs in the upper 30s to 40s Thursday and Friday\nbefore gradually getting above normal again over the weekend.\n\nOutlook... Solid signal (50 to 70%) exists for above normal\ntemperatures through at least the end of the month with El Nino\ndriving the synoptic pattern. Additionally, omega ridge looks to\nset up toward the end of the week, which should result in a\nprolonged period of dry weather to accompany the mild conditions.\nWith D4 drought in far northern Wisconsin, will have to monitor\nfor fire weather concerns over the next several weeks.",
